   "title": "The Siang dam — strategic ambition, ecological fallout",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/lead/the-siang-dam-strategic-ambition-ecological-fallout/article71747632.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Fri, 27 Nov 2026 09:46:06 +0530",
   "_dt": 1795752966.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "The river flows into India as the Siang and subsequently the Brahmaputra. India views SUMP as a potential strategic buffer against upstream hydrological risks. The Siang River, revered as Anni (Mother), holds deep cultural and spiritual significance.",
   "summary_zh": "这条河在印度境内称为锡安河，随后汇入布拉马普特拉河。印度将《萨赫勒-萨赫勒地区水资源综合管理计划》视为抵御上游水文风险 Potential的战略缓冲带。锡安河被尊为阿尼（母亲），具有深厚的文化和精神意义。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The 11,200 MW Siang Upper Multipurpose Project (SUMP) in Arunachal Pradesh has evolved from a decades-old hydropower proposal with less storage capacity into a project with significant strategic and national security implications, particularly in response to China’s decision to proceed with the 60 GW Medog hydropower project on the Yarlung Tsangpo in Tibet. The river flows into India as the Siang and subsequently the Brahmaputra. By 2020, NHPC Limited had intensified preliminary investigations. With a proposed capacity roughly three times that of the Three Gorges Dam, the project has raised concerns in the downstream countries, India and Bangladesh. India views SUMP as a potential strategic buffer against upstream hydrological risks. Its proposed 9.2 billion cubic metres of live storage capacity is expected to regulate excess flows during sudden releases and maintain downstream water availability during periods of reduced discharge. Despite the national security rationale, SUMP faces resistance from indigenous communities. The proposed reservoir could submerge an estimated 27 ancestral villages and displace over 1.5 lakh people, nearly 11% of Arunachal Pradesh’s population. The government’s initial response by deploying armed forces has also deepened tensions. Tribal land protection in northeast India rests on constitutional provisions and statutory safeguards, but their scope varies across States. The Sixth Schedule provides one of the strongest protections through Autonomous District Councils, which exercise legislative and administrative powers over land, customary laws and local governance. However, Arunachal Pradesh, which is not covered by the Sixth Schedule, relies partly on the Arunachal Pradesh Scheduled Tribes (Protection of Transfer of Land) Act, which restricts the transfer of tribal land to non-tribals while allowing government acquisition for public purposes. The key issue, however, is not merely the existence of legal protections but whether they can meaningfully safeguard community rights when large infrastructure projects are undertaken in the name of national security. For the local communities of Arunachal Pradesh, the mountains and valleys provide water, food, medicinal plants and other essential resources while sustaining cultural practices, customary laws and collective identity. The Siang River, revered as Anni (Mother), holds deep cultural and spiritual significance. The valley contains sacred sites, burial grounds and historical landscapes that sustain indigenous traditions and customary institutions. The financial compensation cannot adequately address the consequences of submerging ancestral villages. Their concerns are about losing their social networks, cultural continuity and ecological relationships, and becoming landless communities in unfamiliar areas — an extension of similar stories of existential crisis experienced now by tribal communities in Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh and Great Nicobar. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
