   "title": "Pressure tactics: On the new U.S. tariffs on India",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/editorial/pressure-tactics-on-the-new-us-tariffs-on-india/article71747021.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:59:47 +0530",
   "_dt": 1795746587.0,
   "ageDays": 0.11,
   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The latest U.S. tariffs on India are nothing more than a pressure tactic to push for the signing of a trade deal. The pressure on India does not come from the 25% rate but from the 17% this could be reduced to if it signs a trade deal with the U.S. This latest action is yet another example of how, despite the frothiness of India-U.S. trade relations over the past year and a half, the actual substance has not changed.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容。美国最新对印度征收的关税不过是一种施压手段，旨在推动印度签署贸易协议。印度承受的压力并非来自25%的税率，而是来自印度若与美国签署贸易协议，这17%的税率就可能下调。最新行动再次表明，尽管过去一年半印美贸易关系表面热闹纷繁，其实际实质并未发生改变。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The latest U.S. tariffs on India are nothing more than a pressure tactic to push for the signing of a trade deal. The U.S. Trade Representative (USTR) has proposed to levy a tariff of 25% for India’s apparent use of strategic excess manufacturing capacity to the detriment of American industry. This is the second-highest tariff rate the USTR has applied across the 14 countries that it has taken action against. The pressure on India does not come from the 25% rate but from the 17% this could be reduced to if it signs a trade deal with the U.S. This latest action is yet another example of how, despite the frothiness of India-U.S. trade relations over the past year and a half, the actual substance has not changed. The 25% rate now proposed, which is net of existing tariffs, is nearly the same as the 26% rate that U.S. President Donald Trump had first announced when he unveiled his ‘Liberation Day’ reciprocal tariffs in April 2025. Indeed, it is exactly the same as the 25% tariff he finally imposed in July 2025, before he imposed an additional 25% for India’s Russian oil imports. The 17% rate is also only marginally lower than the 18% that the two countries had agreed on in their joint statement in February 2026. It is also just one percentage point lower than the tariffs on competitors such as Indonesia and Malaysia. The allegation of India having excess capacity does not hold water. Indeed, were India to have excess manufacturing capacity in any sector, it would be the Government of India that would be the first to announce it with great fanfare, instead of denying it as it did. After all, that has been its aim since 2014. Yet, even a basic assessment of India’s import dependence and the fact that manufacturing’s share in GDP is lower now than a decade ago shows that India is far from having excess manufacturing capacity in any sector. Further, higher tariffs are unlikely to reduce America’s imports of the goods from India that it does depend on. The USTR mentioned generic pharmaceuticals, telecommunication equipment, and renewable energy goods. The first two are items that India has developed expertise in, which is distinct from strategic excess capacity. A relatively small tariff disadvantage will not substantially erode the attractiveness of these Indian products. Renewable energy imports from India have swelled because the U.S. is reducing its purchases from China. Hanging over all of this are the 100% tariffs related to the purchase of Russian oil, which have only temporarily been paused. India has so far held off on signing a deal, and must go ahead with one only if it is convinced that the benefits at least equal the costs. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ru",
    "in"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "eco",
    "pol"
   ],
   "tier": 2,
   "actor": "美国",
   "alsoUS": false,
   "cnHit": false,
   "usHit": true,
   "terrHit": false,
   "riskHit": false,
   "riskKind": "",
   "score": 0.6,
   "tag": "rss_thehindu",
   "title_zh": "施压手段：美国对印度征收新关税",
   "body_zh": "订阅账户还可享受《 premium stories 》、社论、观点等更多内容。订阅即可解锁这些内容。美国针对印度的最新关税不过是施压手段，意在推动印度签署贸易协议。美国贸易代表办公室（USTR）提议对印度征收25%的关税，理由是印度似乎利用战略性过剩制造能力损害了美国工业。这是USTR对采取行动的14个国家中实施的第二高关税税率。印度面临的压力并非来自25%的税率，而来自如果印度与美国签署贸易协议，这一税率可降至17%。最新行动再次表明，尽管过去一年半印美贸易关系表面上热闹喧嚣，但实际情况并未改变。如今提议的25%税率是在现有关税基础上净额计算，几乎与美国总统唐纳德·特朗普于2025年4月公布“解放日”互惠关税时最初宣布的26%税率相同。事实上，它与他最终于2025年7月实施的25%关税完全一致；此后，他又因印度进口俄罗斯石油加征了25%的关税。17%的税率也仅略低于两国在2026年2月联合声明中商定的18%。它也只比印度尼西亚和马来西亚等竞争对手所面临的关税低一个百分点。关于印度存在过剩产能的指控并不成立。事实上，如果印度任何行业存在过剩制造能力，那也会是印度政府率先大张旗鼓地宣布，而非像现在这样予以否认。毕竟，自2014年以来，这一直是印度政府的目标。然而，即便只是对印度进口依赖程度以及制造业占国内生产总值比重如今低于十年前这一事实进行基本评估，也足以表明印度远不存在任何行业的过剩制造能力。此外，提高关税不太可能减少美国从印度进口其依赖商品的数量。USTR提到了仿制药、电信设备和可再生能源产品。前两类是印度已经形成专长的产品，这与战略性过剩产能不同。相对较小的关税劣势不会实质性削弱这些印度产品的吸引力。由于美国正减少从中国采购可再生能源产品，来自印度的可再生能源进口有所增加。悬在这一切之上的，是与购买俄罗斯石油有关的100%关税，目前只是暂时暂停。迄今印度一直拒绝签署协议，只有确信收益至少等同于成本时，才会推进签署。评论必须使用英语，并以完整句子表述。评论不得含有辱骂性或人身攻击内容。请遵守我们的社区评论准则。我们已迁移至新的评论平台。如果你已经是《The Hindu》的注册用户并已登录，可以继续参与我们文章的讨论。如果你没有账户，请注册并登录后发表评论。用户可以登录自己的Vuukle账户查看以前的评论",
